round 1: 207 in-scope policy-induced cases, 8 shown; latency 40s, attempts 1, usage 13634
change: {'fdir_abs_Nm': 0.0006} []
diagnosis: FDIR absolute threshold 0.0003 Nm is below mild friction residual ~0.0004 Nm (severity 0.2). It isolates the first mildly-degraded wheel to trip, exhausting the one-isolation budget (min_avail=3). System is left on 3 wheels with the worse fault still present, losing control authority and momentum capacity, causing attitude_loss and wheel_saturation that 4 degraded wheels avoid.
rationale: Raising abs to 0.6 mNm permanently filters severity-0.2 Coulomb faults (~0.4 mNm, observed residuals 4.5-5.1e-04 Nm) while still passing severe friction e.g. 0.68->1.37 mNm. No isolation -> keeps 4-wheel pseudo-inverse control as in without-policy runs which have no violation. True dead/derate wheels still trip when |cmd|>abs, preserving single-fault isolation, only delayed to larger commands. Fixes dominant mild-first mis-isolation and isolation-induced saturation.
risks: Delays isolation of dead/derated wheels until commands exceed 0.6 mNm; may miss moderate faults needing isolation. Single severe friction still detected; nominal/single-mild behavior improved, not degraded.
